<p>Trade Compliance, Logistics Operations, Customs Brokers,</p>

<p style="background:#E8EAF6;border-left:4px solid #283593;padding:12px">
<b>AEO ANNUAL SURVEILLANCE REVIEW — Authorisation AEO-S-11284.</b>
Your Authorized Economic Operator status (Security and Safety, Tier S) enters its annual surveillance
review. The audit team's on-site visit is planned for <b>the second week of next month</b>.
<b>Self-assessment documentation and the updated supply-chain security file must be on file before the
visit</b>; incomplete files at visit time historically result in suspension recommendations, which pause
your fast-lane treatment (reduced scans, priority inspection) for the entire suspension period.
</p>

<h3>1. Authorisation &amp; Review Scope</h3>
<table cellpadding=6 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5;width:260px">Authorisation</td><td><b>AEO-S-11284</b> (Security &amp; Safety, valid to 2028-03)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Holder</td><td>Your company (importer/exporter, 3 bonded facilities)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Review type</td><td>Annual surveillance (desk + 2-day site visit)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Last review</td><td>2026-02 — passed with 3 observations (all closed)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Site visit</td><td>Second week of next month · facilities FC-1 and FC-3</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Reference</td><td>SR-2027-AEO-11284</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2. Documentation Due Before the Visit</h3>
<table cellpadding=5 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr style="background:#f0f0f0"><th>Item</th><th>Scope</th><th>Priority</th></tr>
<tr><td>Self-assessment questionnaire (2027 revision)</td><td>All sections; changes since last review flagged</td><td style="color:#e00">Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business partner security file</td><td>Full vendor list with security screening results, including subcontracted transporters</td><td style="color:#e00">Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security procedures pack</td><td>Access control, seal management, incident response (incl. the October seal-discrepancy follow-up)</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Training records</td><td>AWARE refresher completion for warehouse and driver populations</td><td>High</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3. Submission Portal</h3>
<p>Documentation uploads go through the trader compliance portal, with your session pre-linked to the
review reference:</p>

<p style="text-align:center;margin:18px 0">
<a href="https://trader-compliance.example/aeo/review/SR-2027-AEO-11284/upload" style="display:inline-block;background:#283593;color:#fff;padding:12px 40px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:4px;font-weight:bold">Submit AEO Review Documentation</a>
</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#666;text-align:center">Programme page: https://www.customs.gov.example/traders/aeo (same portal as your original application)</p>

<h3>4. What Suspension Would Mean</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fast-lane treatment paused: full document checks and elevated scan rates on all consignments</li>
<li>Reinstatement requires a fresh on-site audit with full documentation (8–12 weeks)</li>
<li>Downstream: your clients' own AEO chain documentation referencing your authorisation lapses</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14px;background:#E8EAF6;padding:12px">"Customs asking for your full vendor security
file over email" deserves skepticism — the standard route is the government gateway your broker already
uses. Two distinctions here: the submission page is bound to review reference SR-2027-AEO-11284 and shows
your authorisation number and facility list before any upload field; and your customs broker can deposit
the same package through their own portal session if your team prefers. The pre-visit deadline, however,
is set by the audit calendar — files not on record when the team arrives are read as gaps in the
authorisation, not as clerical delay.</p>

